#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Tell the bound owner that an AUTO worker lane on this host finished or died.

    Claude Code hook (Stop, StopFailure, SessionEnd):  sats-completion-notify claude   (hook JSON on stdin)
    Codex notify:                                      sats-completion-notify codex <event JSON>
    systemd ExecStopPost of a lane unit:               sats-completion-notify exit <agent>

It runs only for lane workers: a non-interactive agent (`claude -p`,
`codex exec`) that belongs to a launch recorded in a *.pid file in its pack
(<lane>.pid from the launch recipe or a run-lane script, <lane>.worker.pid
from the old sats-spawn-lane wrapper). Walking up from the agent, the first
recorded pid reached before any other agent process is its launch. Reviewers
and helpers a lane starts reach the lane's agent first, or nothing recorded
when detached, and stay silent, as do interactive sessions. The pack is
SATS_PACK, else the agent's cwd; the lane is SATS_LANE, else the pid file's name.

Neither Stop nor a Codex turn-complete marks the end of a run: Claude resumes
after Stop when background work reports, and `codex exec` can run several
turns, and a launch script may run several agents. So the first event of a
launch claims sent/<run-key>, later events of that launch are dropped, and a
detached child waits on a pidfd until the launched process exits. Then it
reads the verdict and POSTs. The hook itself
returns at once.

A lane launched as a systemd user service (the launch recipe: systemd-run with
SATS_RUN=$(date +%s-%N), the launch time, and ExecStopPost=sats-completion-notify exit)
also reports from ExecStopPost, which runs after every exit of the unit's main
process, SIGKILL included. Both paths use one key per lane run (lane + SATS_RUN)
and post under an flock on outbox/<key>.lock. Only recorded HTTP acceptance
suppresses a later poster. Local DROP/HOLD and failed transmission retain the
body in outbox; all posters share at most three durable attempt reservations.
systemd starts ExecStopPost only after the unit's other processes have exited,
and the native sender blocks SIGTERM, so when a native hook fired its sender
posts first and ExecStopPost rechecks its transport state. ExecStopPost posts alone when no
native hook ran: SIGKILL, SIGTERM during a tool call, any Codex crash.

The payload is one JSON object:
  {lane, pack, host, agent, verdict, reason, ts_ct, test, event, run, result_file,
   outcome, step, step_name, result_verdict, result_fresh, review_verdict, rerun,
   event_id, event_kind, review_round, notice_occurrence, delivery_contract,
   required_notice, recipient, priority}
The prepared contract routes a launch-bound notice to its exact recipient;
unbound and TEST lanes keep the legacy filter path. HTTP acceptance
does not establish native recipient delivery. Start/decision/blocker/review notices
need separate owner-integrated emitters and an attested native receiver.
A launch script may run several steps (producer, then a review or a retry), and
the report comes when the whole launch ends. The step that ran last is the
newer of sent/<run-key>.step (SATS_STEP of each native event of the launch,
exported per step by run-lane.sh) and the last step line of the pack's lane.log
("<step> start", "<step> exit N", a repair loop's "repair rN <step> exit N")
written since the start, or review when a REVIEW-SOL*.md written since the start
is newer than both; with neither, the lane name's -review/-retry suffix; else
SATS_STEP of the poster.
result_verdict is the first PASS/FAIL/BLOCKED/PARTIAL line, else the line under
a "Verdict" heading or after "Verdict:", of the newest RESULT* in the pack,
preferring one written since the start (result_fresh). Any mtime counts.
review_verdict is ACCEPT or REQUEST_CHANGES when the first non-blank line of the
review's output, trimmed of spaces and tabs, starts with that word followed by an
ASCII word boundary (ACCEPTED and ACCEPT_X are neither; ACCEPT: and ACCEPT² are
ACCEPT; the repair loops' LC_ALL=C verdict() uses the same rule): the newest
REVIEW-SOL*.md written since the start (else REVIEW*), the higher round at equal
mtimes; else null.
outcome and verdict:
  DONE            a review step whose review_verdict is ACCEPT: "ACCEPTED: step
                  review (REVIEW-SOL.r2), exit 0, review ACCEPT. RESULT-x.md: ...".
                  Also a producer step and a RESULT written since the start:
                  verdict is result_verdict, or a note that the RESULT has no verdict line.
  REVIEW / RETRY  the last step was a review or retry: "REVIEW STOP: step review
                  (REVIEW-SOL), exit 0. RESULT-x.md: PASS ...". REQUEST_CHANGES adds
                  "changes requested, repair then review again". A producer step
                  whose only RESULT predates the start is a RETRY stop too.
  DIED            no RESULT* in the pack at all, whatever step ran last (a review
                  run-lane.sh starts after a dead producer reviews nothing).
  UNKNOWN         the classifier failed (logged in errors.log); the report still goes.
rerun names what to run again: "producer" only for DIED, "step:<step>" for a
review or retry step with no review_verdict that exited non-zero (lane.log, else
the unit's exit for ExecStopPost), was cut off, or left its REVIEW* output empty
(or, for a review, missing), else "none". A review with a verdict never re-runs,
and a lane with a RESULT never gets "producer". Bound notices send event_id as
the Idempotency-Key header; other lanes send the run key.

Delivery goes to the Grok routine webhook stored in 1Password item
"Grok completion webhook (Lightning)" (fields url, key), read with sats-op
and cached 0600 under ~/.local/state/sats-completion/. Redirects are refused
so the key never leaves for another origin. Tests may point at a loopback
listener with SATS_COMPLETION_WEBHOOK_URL / SATS_COMPLETION_WEBHOOK_KEY.

Native hooks are fail-open: their exit status is always 0. Once an explicit
milestone has durable local proof, its CLI exits 0 and retains delivery
failure in a private machine-readable receipt. Local errors remain nonzero.
Stalled events append only private state, never a lane heartbeat.
Errors go to errors.log as exception
type and code locations only, never messages, so the URL and key cannot leak.
"""

